<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Berar Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1490 CE – 1574 CE · capital Ellichpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The smallest of the Deccan sultanates, ruled by the Imad Shahi dynasty from Ellichpur in the Berar plain of central India.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fathullah-imad-ul-mulk">Fathullah Imad-ul-Mulk</a>, a Bahmani governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> who was originally a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> convert from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara</a> territory, declared independence around 1490 as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> broke up. He ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achalpur">Ellichpur</a> and the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gawilgarh">Gawilgarh</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-shahi-dynasty">Imad Shahi dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> was a fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and grain-growing region, but the sultanate was weak and survived by balancing between its neighbours, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ahmadnagar-sultanate">Ahmadnagar Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khandesh-sultanate">Khandesh Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate</a>. Its rulers strengthened the hill fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gawilgarh">Gawilgarh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narnala">Narnala</a>. Few literary sources describe its internal history.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murtaza-nizam-shah-i">Murtaza Nizam Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ahmadnagar-sultanate">Ahmadnagar Sultanate</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> and annexed it in 1574, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-shahi-dynasty">Imad Shahi</a> line. Berar later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizams of Hyderabad</a> and finally the British.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/berar-sultanate.jpg" alt="Berar Sultanate" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Mr.Hanes · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berar_Sultanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1490 CE – 1574 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ellichpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fathullah Imad-ul-Mulk, Alauddin Imad Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Imad Shahi dynasty, Imad Shahis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=berar-sultanate" aria-label="Berar Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6763 1392 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Berar Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7083 1648L7088 1790M7083 1648L7021 1726"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7088 1790h0M7021 1726h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7083 1648h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7083 1648h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=berar-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
